@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Performance Guidelines";
}
@section css
{
    @Styles.Render("~/bundles/controls/css/aspmvc")
}

<div class="col-sm-12" id="doc-content">
    <ul class="rule-list">
        <li><a href="#" class="link-sub-title" code="OW7000">OW7000 Consider using <small>Any()</small> to determine whether an <small>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</small> is empty</a></li>
        <li><a href="#" class="link-sub-title" code="OW7001">OW7001 Only use <small>async</small> for low-intensive long-running activities</a></li>
        <li><a href="#" class="link-sub-title" code="OW7002">OW7002 Prefer <small>Task.Run</small> for CPU intensive activities</a></li>
        <li><a href="#" class="link-sub-title" code="OW7003">OW7003 Beware of mixing up <small>await/async</small> with <small>Task.Wait</small></a></li>
        <li><a href="#" class="link-sub-title" code="OW7004">OW7004 Beware of <small>async/await</small> deadlocks in single-threaded environments</a></li>
    </ul>
    <ul class="rules unordered">
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title first" id="OW7000"><span class="rule-item">OW7000 </span> Consider using <small>Any()</small> to determine whether an <small>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</small> is empty <span class="lbl-importance">*</span></h2>
            <p>
                When a method or other member returns an IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; or other collection class that does not expose a Count property, 
                use the <i>Any()</i> extension method rather than <i>Count()</i> to determine whether the collection contains items. If you do use <i>Count()</i>, 
                you risk that iterating over the entire collection might have a significant impact (such as when it really is an <i>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</i> to a persistent store).
            </p>
            <div class="note-content">
                <div class="note-title note">Note</div>
                <ul class="note-list">
                    <li>
                        If you return an <i>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</i> to prevent editing from outside the owner as explained in
                        <span class="text-info">OW3004</span> and you’re developing in .NET 4.5 or higher,
                        consider the new read-only classes.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title" id="OW7001"><span class="rule-item">OW7001 </span> Only use <small>async</small>  for low-intensive long-running activities</h2>
            <p>
                The usage of <i>async</i> won’t auto magically run something on a worker thread like <i>Task.Run</i> does. 
                It just adds the necessary logic to allow releasing the current thread and marshal the result back on that same thread if a long-running asynchronies operation has completed. 
                In other words, use <i>async</i> only for I/O bound operations.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title" id="OW7002"><span class="rule-item">OW7002 </span> Prefer <small>Task.Run</small> for CPU intensive activities</h2>
            <p>
                If you do need to execute a CPU bound operation, use <i>Task.Run</i> to offload the work to a thread from the Thread Pool. <br />
                Just don’t forget that you have to marshal the result back to your main thread manually.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title" id="OW7003"><span class="rule-item">OW7003 </span> Beware of mixing up <small>await/async</small> with <small>Task.Wait</small></h2>
            <p>
                <i>Await</i> not block the current thread but simply instruct to compiler to generate a state-machine. However,
                <i>Task.Wait</i> will block the thread and may even cause dead-locks (see <span class="text-info">OW7004</span>)
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title" id="OW7004"><span class="rule-item">OW7004 </span> Beware of <small>async/await</small> deadlocks in single-threaded environments</h2>
            <div class="example-code none-top-content">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    private async Task&lt;string&gt; GetDataAsync()
                    {
                        var result = await MyWebService.GetDataAsync();
                        return result.ToString();
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>Consider the following asynchronous method:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    public ActionResult ActionAsync()
                    {
                        var data = GetDataAsync().Result;
                        return View(data);
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
            <p>Now When an ASP.NET MVC controller action does this:
                You’ll end up with a deadlock. Why? Because the Result property getter will block until the <i>async</i> operation has completed, 
                but since an <i>async</i> method will automatically marshal the result back to the original thread and ASP.NET uses a single-threaded synchronization context, 
                they’ll be waiting on each other. </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>
